he didn't actually manage to resurrect completely, it's just that some sort of echos of him possessed people. But it felt like an entirely valid interpretation that at no point during actual gameplay was there an actual, awake, thinking Sephiroth; he died years ago and every time you saw him, it was just mindless echos of him that occasionally took over people who shared his genes or whatever the hell it was. As I recall, even after he theoretically reassembles himself, he doesn't have a single line in the &quot;real world&quot; as opposed to in people's heads.<br /> <br /> I could be forgetting something? But I'm pretty sure he never does or says anything that would imply that he's sentient in the modern day, or in any way cognizant of what's happening outside of a few reflexive instincts to attack certain people and grab / use certain things. Like, seriously, Exdeath was a less wooden character, and he was <i>literally a tree</i>.<br /> <br /> But more generally, I felt that Midgar was the only remotely interesting or fleshed-out location in the game. None of the scenes set outside of Midgar were remotely as effective to me as the ones inside it because of that; the rest of the world was pretty much just generic Final Fantasy places.</div></div> </div> </div></div> </div> </div> </div><div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">I think the reason the game isn't about that is it's too real. Basically, you're playing a hyper-violent version of Occupy Wall Street+Greenpeace. You're fighting corporate greed crushing the majority of people, sometimes literally, for its own good. They own the police. They own any politicians. And... a lot of people want to, at least once in a while, play games to get away from the awfulness of real life and relax in a story about playing people who CAN win. The heroes can't beat Shinra. Everything they're doing? Never really scratched the company. Sephiroth? He's a more possible enemy to defeat compared to the multi-headed monster that is a corporation grinding up the people to feed its gluttonous hunger for more money and power.<br /> <br /> I'm all about fighting those structures. But I'd rather focus those efforts in the real world, and save my game time for something more relaxing.<br /> <br /> tldr; You're playing a progressive Social Justice Warrior at the start of Final Fantasy VII. You're not when you're fighting Sephiroth. Or not as much.</div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />\u200bWhen noise turns to silence, when colors dull and pale, when reality no longer makes sense, there shall you find me. There, in the dreams of the River of Faceless Millions, do I dwell.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/\" target=\"_blank\">http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/</a> for my art.","edited":{"userid":378,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","user":"haren","date":".  More Clarification 04-17-2016, 09:26 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","name":"haren","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar378_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"664"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T06:36:15-07:00","id":866791,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aquillion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=866690#post866690\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">having Kejak be, canonically, Literally Gaddafi is not good for the game</span></span></span>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Why not?<br /> <br /> The Scarlet Empress doesn't prevent Dragon-Blooded player characters from being heroic.<br /> <br /> Unless you meant that Kejak's resources and the low number of Sidereals would make it harder to successfully rebel, if he were a paranoid, power-hungry tyrant.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>formerly Tornado Wolf, formerly Inugami","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","name":"Sunder the Gold","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar636_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5515"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T06:40:49-07:00","id":866794,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>haren</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=866783#post866783\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">I think the reason the game isn't about that is it's too real. Basically, you're playing a hyper-violent version of Occupy Wall Street. You're fighting corporate greed crushing the majority of people, sometimes literally, for its own good. They own the police. They own any politicians. And... a lot of people want to, at least once in a while, play games to get away from the awfulness of real life and relax in a story about playing people who CAN win. The heroes can't beat Shinra. Everything they're doing? Never really scratched the company. Sephiroth? He's a more possible enemy to defeat compared to the multi-headed monster that is a corporation grinding up the people to feed its gluttonous hunger for more money and power.<br /> <br /> I'm all about fighting those structures. But I'd rather focus those efforts in the real world, and save my game time for something more relaxing.</div></div> </div> </div></div> </div> </div> </div>That seems like a lazy answer to me. Telling a story about how the heroes beat real-world problems rather than just beating up superdemons is absolutely <i>harder</i>, but that difficulty comes with a correspondingly higher payoff when you manage it. You can still have it be an escapist story if that's what you want! You can have the heroes win. It's harder for the writer, though, because the players will know if you do it cheaply.<br /> <br /> But, like... Suikoden manages it? Mostly? You can tell interesting stories about beating human-scale threats. I think part of the problem is also this desire for stories of <i>absolute</i> victory; it's not enough to beat an enemy nation or to make the world better in some way, it's like games need to make your conflict into an ultimate confrontation between good and evil that resolves the entire setting.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I find the idea that game writers (and, I guess, storytellers?) should focus on generic superdemon plots because those plots are <i>easy</i> to be saddening somehow.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","name":"Aquillion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar517_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1341"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T06:46:54-07:00","id":866800,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aquillion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=866794#post866794\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That seems like a lazy answer to me. Telling a story about how the heroes beat real-world problems rather than just beating up superdemons is absolutely <i>harder</i>, but that difficulty comes with a correspondingly higher payoff when you manage it. You can still have it be an escapist story if that's what you want! You can have the heroes win. It's harder for the writer, though, because the players will know if you do it cheaply.<br /> <br /> But, like... Suikoden manages it? Mostly? You can tell interesting stories about beating human-scale threats. I think part of the problem is also this desire for stories of <i>absolute</i> victory; it's not enough to beat an enemy nation or to make the world better in some way, it's like games need to make your conflict into an ultimate confrontation between good and evil that resolves the entire setting.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I find the idea that game writers (and, I guess, storytellers?) should focus on generic superdemon plots because those plots are <i>easy</i> to be saddening somehow.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Hey, you want more games about fighting for Social Justice, I'll be the first person standing beside you there. It's a good thing! But sometimes I also want a game where I don't have to think/feel hard on those days that just have sucked the life from me. I want to just enjoy the game's physical aspects... maybe even some minor strategy. I want a challenge, but more of a somewhat physical nature.<br /> <br /> Still, it's nice to see more people wanting social justice in the world. It warms your heart. Also, it shows people are admitting politics are everywhere, just some support the status quo rather than fighting it. That's it, Social Justice Abyssals! You're a genius Aquillion! To bring it back to the original topic of the thread.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />\u200bWhen noise turns to silence, when colors dull and pale, when reality no longer makes sense, there shall you find me. There, in the dreams of the River of Faceless Millions, do I dwell.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/\" target=\"_blank\">http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/</a> for my art.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","name":"haren","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar378_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"664"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T06:51:15-07:00","id":866802,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sunder the Gold</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=866791#post866791\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why not?<br /> <br /> The Scarlet Empress doesn't prevent Dragon-Blooded player characters from being heroic.<br /> <br /> Unless you meant that Kejak's resources and the low number of Sidereals would make it harder to successfully rebel, if he were a paranoid, power-hungry tyrant.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think part of why this keeps going back to Kejak specifically is that we know frustratingly little about the internal structure of the Bronze Faction. They've got so few NPCs that it feels like the Gold massively outnumbers them rather than the other way around.<br /> <br /> It occurs to me that this might actually be where Rakan Thulio can do some good.<br /> A lot of people's ideas of the Bronze Faction seem to have grown out of the idea of Kejak as an antediluvian schemer who has no equals and his mere presence gives his faction an overwhelming advantage. The existence of an active Sidereal who is considerably older than him is a bit of a shakeup.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":7,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","user":"Lioness","date":". 04-17-2016, 10:15 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T07:19:18-07:00","id":866816,"title":"","content":"Oooooooooblivion! Let's walk through just what kind of antagonist it really is.<br /> <br /> Is it a personal antagonist like Sylar, who can shoot your dog and kidnap your wife and have a gritty yet tragic past that the writer seems to think excuses anything? Fuck no, it's a black hole, it doesn't care about you or anything you do.<br /> <br /> Okay! Is it a horror antagonist like the Great Old Ones or the Reapers (before Harbinger became an xbox live kid), where the point is you are so insignificant in the face of it that it doesn't even notice you or identify you as any less than an ant and you just sort of have to deal with it? Well yes, except that you <i>aren't</i> insignificant by virtue of being one of the Exalted and Exalted doesn't <i>do</i> horror, it's sort of antiethical to the whole thesis. I think there's even a writer quote lying around somewhere to the effect of, &quot;hey writer, how inevitable is Oblivion, really?&quot; &quot;it's not. At all. Nephwracks are fuckin' bonkers.&quot;<br /> <br /> Well okay then! How about, is it an elemental force of nature that you must struggle against or survive against or mitigate, like outrunning global warming in the Day After Tomorrow? Nooot really. It drives ghosts crazy sometimes, but other than that your chances of getting fucked over by it are basically nil. Try not to jump into the giant fucking black hole in the middle of the Labyrinth, a place you really shouldn't be going anyway.<br /> <br /> Okay! Is it an elemental antagonist like Lavos, who you have to fight and overcome and who drives the plot by the reactions of other people to it? That sounds the most accurate, except you can't actually 'fight' Oooooblivion, or really even affect it because again, it's a black hole. Also, while the plot of Chrono Trigger is kicked off when Lavos destroys the world, Oblivion hasn't <i>done</i> anything yet, it's just sort of abstractly scary at some point in the distant future or when the deathknights fulfill a grand murder-plan that probably won't even work.<br /> <br /> The fundamental problem with Oblivion is that it manages to peg an entire section of playable characters as generally awful without some serious rewriting (because what kind of dumbass would tirelessly work to bring about an omnicide that <i>includes him</i>?) while at the same time it's neither fish, nor fowl, nor foal, nor the heat death of the universe, winding up as just about the most ineffectual faceless elemental villain that's ever been. It's just sort of... <i>there</i>. Vampire Nic Cage is scarier than Oblivion.<br /> <br /> And, I mean, jegus, if you for some reason <i>needed</i> omnicide as an endgame, at least let the stupid bastards figure out a way to make a new world in their own image. You'd have <i>lots</i> of takers for ultramurder if that was the carrot! If you had a horse with a broken leg and it was the only horse left in your corner of the world you bet your fucking <i>ass</i> you'd try to save it, fragile or no.<br /> <br /> If you need to keep the mass murder, let the Abyssals be the &quot;good guys&quot; in their own heads that way. They're going to make a paradise or at least something better, just as soon as they murder everyone. It'd still be <i>wrong</i>, but it would at least be the right sort of wrong. (It'd be a cliche, yes, but are you going to tell me &quot;villain who kills everyone and wants to end it all&quot; or &quot;villain who seemed like a villain but was really keeping back a greater evil&quot; is sparkling fresh?)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2144,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2144-guancyto","user":"Guancyto","date":". 04-17-2016, 10:24 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2144-guancyto","name":"Guancyto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"699"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T07:48:46-07:00","id":866827,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ghosthead</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=866745#post866745\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">for FF6 you could also say it would have been just as interesting to deal with the political aftermath of Kefka's destruction of the world. What new powers form in the apocalyptic aftermath? What is the Cult of Ruin, exactly? How is this all informed by the world that came before? Rather than seeing that as a detracting shift.) </div></div> </div> </div></div> </div> </div> </div><b>Final Fantasy 6</b><br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">If you wanted to explore apocalyptic aftermath, FF6 is ALREADY a post-apocalyptic world at the start of the game. The world as you know exists in the shadow of the War of Magi.<br /> <br /> Yes, I know it's different when you personally had explored the length and breadth of the world before it was ruined, but recall that the ending of FF6 actually goes so far as to <i>push the reset button</i> on the world after you kill Kefka. Even as a kid, I thought that was weird, because it didn't make logical sense. It couldn't be because you released the magic that Kekfa stole back into the world, because all of the magic disappeared when he died!<br /> <br /> That's another thing I resent. Rather than explore a world where people have to make mature decisions about how to use magic without abusing magic and exploiting the people who can use it, Squaresoft just threw the entire issue of magic out of the door.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> On the other hand, I can see the appeal of a videogame that had the kind of iconic artistry in character design, world design, monsters, art, music, scenario, mood as a classic Final Fantasy game, with the kind of choice and complicated politics and large scale strategy found in other videogames. It's a big ask though. </div> </div> </div>I think Aquillion is telling us that <i>Suikoden </i>managed it. At least TWICE.<br /> <br /> Besides, it's not like FF6 needed to forsake &quot;punching the bad guy in the face&quot;. Like I said, there were still punchable bad guys in the forms of General Leo and Emperor Gestahl... also a non-god Kefka, if the emperor can control him enough that he doesn't need to execute him.<br /> <br /> It's just that the story could explore the fact that eventually, punching dudes in the face stops being the best solution to the problem. That's where CUT SCENES happen, like all of the cut scenes that happened previously in FF6. The cut scenes remain engaging and evocative until it's time to punch someone in the face again.<br /> <br /> Or until you need to perform one of FF6's wonderfully organic mini-games which never felt like mini-games. Like Locke's 'Tactical Espionage Action' in South Figaro, or the back-stage battle during the opera where Celes is posing as the diva, or how Celes needs to hunt by hand for healthy, meaty fish at the beach in order to nurse her father away from Death's door.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aquillion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=866772#post866772\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have no idea why people think Sephiroth is an interesting character. Hell, I have no idea why people think he <i>is</i> a character, he has like three lines.<br /> <br /> Like, seriously, Exdeath was a less wooden character, and he was <i>literally a tree</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>I totally agree. Sephiroth stopped being a person long ago, and just became this elemental force of Evil and Destruction. Fighting him was like fighting a tornado or earthquake. Especially because Square-Enix has made it clear to me that they're just going to keep bringing the bastard back like Ganondorf.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But more generally, I felt that Midgar was the only remotely interesting or fleshed-out location in the game. None of the scenes set outside of Midgar were remotely as effective to me as the ones inside it because of that; the rest of the world was pretty much just generic Final Fantasy places.<br /> <br /> (I have other issues regarding it being about your character's relationships, though. I felt that that its development of those collapsed after you left Midgar the first time and were able to select your party; from that point on, there was almost no interaction between anyone in your party and anyone except Cloud. This is common, but it's still irritating. The only characters who get any real characterization outside of their one designated sidequest are Cloud, Aeris, Tifa, and Barret, and most of that is in the early game when they knew who would be in your party. I replayed the game recently and it was <i>extremely noticeable</i> how the density of characterization and plot collapsed, sharply, after you left Midgar, replaced with long stretches of just going from place to place. But this is a bit off-topic, because that issue doesn't really have much to do with Exalted or tabletop games in general. Still, it annoys me how many games fall into that trap where the characterization suddenly stops or slows to a crawl once you can control who's in your party... or just adding tons of characters at the expense of fleshing them out.) </div> </div> </div>This reminds me of a specialized review of the <i>Sonic: The Hedgehog</i> games which examined ONLY the first stage of each game.<br /> <br /> Just as with Sega and <i>Sonic: The Hedgehog</i>, Squaresoft and Square-Enix need to draw you into the game with the first level, or else you'll immediately return the game to the store for a refund. So the studio puts the LION'S share of their time and effort into the start of the game, which necessarily leaves less energy for dealing with the rest of the game... and even less time when the company refuses to adjust the deadline, or even insists on moving the deadline up.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>haren</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=866783#post866783\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">tldr; You're playing a progressive Social Justice Warrior at the start of Final Fantasy VII. You're not when you're fighting Sephiroth. Or not as much.</div> </div> </div> </div>So do what <i>Kingdom Hearts</i> did.<br /> <br /> Have Sephiroth visit (and destroy) Nifleheim when Cloud and Tifa are still just kids, spurring Cloud and Tifa to chase after him in revenge and eventually gathering a party of other heroes who want to save the world.<br /> <br /> If that was the story you wanted to tell, why EVER bother with Midgar and the Shinra Corporation? You just wasted time!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>formerly Tornado Wolf, formerly Inugami","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","name":"Sunder the Gold","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar636_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5515"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T08:11:09-07:00","id":866836,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sunder the Gold</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=866827#post866827\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I totally agree. Sephiroth stopped being a person long ago, and just became this elemental force of Evil and Destruction. Fighting him was like fighting a tornado or earthquake. Especially because Square-Enix has made it clear to me that they're just going to keep bringing the bastard back like Ganondorf.<br /> <br /> So do what <i>Kingdom Hearts</i> did.<br /> <br /> Have Sephiroth visit (and destroy) Nifleheim when Cloud and Tifa are still just kids, spurring Cloud and Tifa to chase after him in revenge and eventually gathering a party of other heroes who want to save the world.<br /> <br /> If that was the story you wanted to tell, why EVER bother with Midgar and the Shinra Corporation? You just wasted time!</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, I suppose because Shinra created Sephiroth. I don't mean figuratively. I mean LITEREALLY they made him. And if you want to point to they didn't &quot;make him evil&quot;... nope, Hojo did that again, who was a bigwig in Shinra. Now, do I think you're so much wrong, nah. I'm just saying, if you squint, maybe what they did makes some sort of sense... you might need some headtilting to help make it work.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />\u200bWhen noise turns to silence, when colors dull and pale, when reality no longer makes sense, there shall you find me. There, in the dreams of the River of Faceless Millions, do I dwell.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/\" target=\"_blank\">http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/</a> for my art.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","name":"haren","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar378_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"664"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T08:14:42-07:00","id":866839,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aquillion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=866772#post866772\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">(FF7 + FF5 spoilers.)<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">I totally did think that, no joking. I mean, I didn't think I'd <i>control</i> it or make those choices, but I thought the plot would be primarily about fighting Shinra. I went into the game without being spoiled on it at all, and my reaction for most of the second half of the first disk was &quot;when is this sideplot about chasing around a black-cloaked man going to end so we can get back to fighting Shinra?&quot;<br /> <br /> I have no idea why people think Sephiroth is an interesting character. Hell, I have no idea why people think he <i>is</i> a character, he has like three lines. Does he even say anything in the last part of the game when you fight him? I don't think he does. I'm not sure he's still even sentient at that point. Honestly I'm not sure he was sentient at any point after being killed and falling into the lifestream, he was just sort of an... echo-thing? It wasn't very clear. My reading is that he didn't actually manage to resurrect completely, it's just that some sort of echos of him possessed people. But it felt like an entirely valid interpretation that at no point during actual gameplay was there an actual, awake, thinking Sephiroth; he died years ago and every time you saw him, it was just mindless echos of him that occasionally took over people who shared his genes or whatever the hell it was. As I recall, even after he theoretically reassembles himself, he doesn't have a single line in the &quot;real world&quot; as opposed to in people's heads.<br /> <br /> I could be forgetting something? But I'm pretty sure he never does or says anything that would imply that he's sentient in the modern day, or in any way cognizant of what's happening outside of a few reflexive instincts to attack certain people and grab / use certain things. Like, seriously, Exdeath was a less wooden character, and he was <i>literally a tree</i>.<br /> <br /> But more generally, I felt that Midgar was the only remotely interesting or fleshed-out location in the game. None of the scenes set outside of Midgar were remotely as effective to me as the ones inside it because of that; the rest of the world was pretty much just generic Final Fantasy places.<br /> <br /> (I have other issues regarding it being about your character's relationships, though. I felt that that its development of those collapsed after you left Midgar the first time and were able to select your party; from that point on, there was almost no interaction between anyone in your party and anyone except Cloud. This is common, but it's still irritating. The only characters who get any real characterization outside of their one designated sidequest are Cloud, Aeris, Tifa, and Barret, and most of that is in the early game when they knew who would be in your party. I replayed the game recently and it was <i>extremely noticeable</i> how the density of characterization and plot collapsed, sharply, after you left Midgar, replaced with long stretches of just going from place to place. But this is a bit off-topic, because that issue doesn't really have much to do with Exalted or tabletop games in general. Still, it annoys me how many games fall into that trap where the characterization suddenly stops or slows to a crawl once you can control who's in your party... or just adding tons of characters at the expense of fleshing them out.)</div></div> </div> </div></div> </div> </div> </div>I don't actually disagree too much with that...<br /> (still FF7)<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"> Sephi is not an interesting guy. When I think about it objectively. Oh, he has a cool and interesting *backstory* - super soldier with mysterious origins as a mad science project, who goes past the brink of sanity on discovering this - is generally badass longcoat incarnate in his introduction. He's the sting in the tail of Shinra's (and man's) dabbling with the legacy of Cetra and the Lifestream, forces beyond them, manifest in their ravaging of the world. He's the point that ties together Shinra, the SOLDIER (pointless acronym!) program and the protagonist, Cloud.<br /> <br /> But then, you don't get any plot development with him. His motive is pretty much &quot;My mum is Lavos; your planet is invalid&quot; (to the degree he's not a dead, mindless force). Teenage (just) me is pretty impressed, adult me is less impressed.<br /> <br /> I think it still stands as a better game than it would have been if it were about fighting the corporate power that's crushing the people and killing the planet, man! (For me I guess, over thinking it, perhaps because there's something sort of a little ridiculous about viewing the Shinra as specifically an external dominating corporate power structure to be railed against, rather than just the worldly manifestation of all the tendencies we have within us to overconsume, to desecrate our traditions and to desecrate the natural world for profit, lifestyle and convenience, that gets humbled by the consequences of its own arrogance and diminished in the world).<br /> <br /> I don't really agree about Midgar so much. Cosmo Canyon, Golden Saucer, Wutai, Nibelheim, the rocket launch site, the ruined town of Coral, all left an impression. Generally in my memories, the rest of the world kind of enriched the game because you had this total contrast between this quite natural, yet empty, sparsely populated world, and this huge, overpopulated city that's unnaturally out of context and way too technologically overgrown for it and sucking in all the energy and activity of the world. (I guess I also feel like that comes from the Japanese experience of industrialisation, and patterns of development in their country, exaggerated. but I might be overlabouring it. In reality it's just as likely or more likely Square's budget <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> ). I haven't replayed it for a long while though, so it has the polish of nostalgia still.<br /> <br /> FF7 remake is, I reckon, totally going to be about tons of Midgar though. (If it ends up working as a game at all!).<br /> <br /> Character development between the party, yes, pretty much is very basic, and mainly carried by their charisma and the archetypes and mentally filling in the gaps, and their one character development sidequest each. While that's preferable to these stories that have a lot of filler of sort of continuous childish squabbling between the party as a sign that, hey, there's Conflict! and interaction going on here, I pretty much agree that I think you'd have a much better story about how the relationships develop over time if it was actually planned who was interacting and when (that just wasn't the priority then, I guess). I'm thinking of FF games classically having a personal relationship focus as the ideal though, more than how well it was realised due to having character swapping and pretty basic storytelling at times.</div></div> </div> </div> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sunder the Gold</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=866827#post866827\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think Aquillion is telling us that <i>Suikoden </i>managed it. At least TWICE.</div> </div> </div> </div>Suikoden didn't get iconic in the same way though. Maybe that's just a sign I (and more people generally) should've played more Suikoden.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T08:27:19-07:00","id":866847,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>haren</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=866836#post866836\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, I suppose because Shinra created Sephiroth. I don't mean figuratively. I mean LITEREALLY they made him. And if you want to point to they didn't &quot;make him evil&quot;... nope, Hojo did that again, who was a bigwig in Shinra. Now, do I think you're so much wrong, nah. I'm just saying, if you squint, maybe what they did makes some sort of sense... you might need some headtilting to help make it work.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, I reject the premise.<br /> <br /> Did Sora start his story in Radiant Garden, where Ansem came from? No, Sora visits &quot;Hollow Bastion&quot; during the course of his adventure.<br /> <br /> Visiting Midgar could have been the same.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>formerly Tornado Wolf, formerly Inugami","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","name":"Sunder the Gold","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar636_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5515"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T09:13:03-07:00","id":866865,"title":"","content":"I think one of the advantages of Exalted is that although it doesn't handwave sociopolitics, it's also set at a point in technology and population where dealing with nations isn't an unwieldy mess. If you've got a problem with a neighboring country, it's entirely possible that what you need to do is set out and have a talk with its king, or some of his companions. Outside of the Realm you can generally get enough individuals and goals to fill a cast list without feeling bare-bones. Also why Suikoden has the advantage of being about wars in a time of kings, and the plot would tend to involve some sort of personal betrayal regarding said war.<br /> <br /> Edit: The animism helps a lot too! To end a drought you might have to perform a convoluted series of favor for an important god, but there's <i>someone you can talk to about the drought</i>. Don't underestimate that.<br /> <br /> (This is also why media that delves into political intrigue <i>loves</i> medieval or renaissance eras - the politics are personal enough to be relatable. Also why House of Cards enormously misrepresents the American political process - because watching Kevin Spacey sit in a cubicle phoning people for donations for six hours, or spend 75% of his time at fund-raising events, or sit on a completely meaningless series of committee hearings for a week because one of his allies wanted to make a point... none of that would be compelling.)<br /> <br /> The Final Fantasy adage of &quot;every problem in the world can be solved by finding the right long-haired prettyboy and beating him up&quot; is... well it's not a <i>great</i> technique but it's an effective one for making things memorable at least, to concentrate your antagonism into something you can grasp and beat the crap out of. FFXIII attempted a lot of what you're describing (but, you know, with Square-Enix at the helm) and... what happened in that game again?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2144,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2144-guancyto","user":"Guancyto","date":". 04-17-2016, 03:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2144-guancyto","name":"Guancyto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"699"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T10:07:28-07:00","id":866899,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Guancyto</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=866865#post866865\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Final Fantasy adage of &quot;every problem in the world can be solved by finding the right long-haired prettyboy and beating him up&quot; is... well it's not a <i>great</i> technique but it's an effective one for making things memorable at least, to concentrate your antagonism into something you can grasp and beat the crap out of. FFXIII attempted a lot of what you're describing (but, you know, with Square-Enix at the helm) and... what happened in that game again?</div> </div> </div> </div>Final Fantasy FF XIV spoilers...<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">I'm not sure if FF XIII is even a game. Seriously, it's a &quot;game&quot; where you don't really choose how you fight. You just tell someone a VERY general thing of like &quot;Go bruiser, or healer, or whatever&quot; and sit back and watch the fight... or watch the hours and hours of cutscenes.<br /> <br /> I think FF XIV is a better example that you CAN do Final Fantasy AND do politics (though it is an MMO). I mean, part of the whole story is the fact that your goals and those of the leaders of countries that are your allies... don't align that much. And that they want you to do really fucking questionable shit for them sometimes. &quot;Oh hey, so... these people summoned their angry god that your group is dedicated to destroying because they hurt the world.&quot; &quot;They only summoned it because you broke the treaty with them and stole land.&quot; &quot;MAAAAYYYBE. But it's still your job to kill the god.&quot;<br /> <br /> And that's just ONE of many political storylines you're part of. Sure, it's on rails, but it's one where you have a lot of different impact. Also, there are non-straight people all over the place. A couple of questgivers are a guy couple. One merchant during an event talked about how she HATES her sweater, but her wife made it for her, so she wears it. The world is complex and a lot of those things are there... but you beat more than a few of them. Evil dude murdering indigenous people for his undead army? Blow up the crucible he's making the potion to make undead and arrest him and his minions. A lot of the badness has a real world vibe and you DO fight it. Sometimes you win, sometimes you lose. But you fight on.<br /> <br /> Also, I love Dark Knights. Warriors in a medieval Catholic society where nobility and clergy can do no wrong. They go unpunished. Sometimes there are people who are so angry, so filled with righteous rage that they harness it. Become pariahs in their own land to beat down those who would otherwise be above the law, because they ARE the law.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sunder the Gold</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=866847#post866847\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, I reject the premise.<br /> <br /> Did Sora start his story in Radiant Garden, where Ansem came from? No, Sora visits &quot;Hollow Bastion&quot; during the course of his adventure.<br /> <br /> Visiting Midgar could have been the same.</div> </div> </div> </div>Note, I'm not arguing their choices were right. Just that, maybe, if you look hard, they had reasons. Other than it's easy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />\u200bWhen noise turns to silence, when colors dull and pale, when reality no longer makes sense, there shall you find me. There, in the dreams of the River of Faceless Millions, do I dwell.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/\" target=\"_blank\">http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/</a> for my art.","edited":{"userid":378,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","user":"haren","date":". 04-17-2016, 01:09 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","name":"haren","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar378_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"664"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T12:18:15-07:00","id":866985,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=866727#post866727\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">These mostly (not sure about the last) seem like good motivations for an Abyssal generally. I don't really see them as being part of a plan to subvert Oblivion though, they're just independent of it.<br /> Which is, in my opinion, a good thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>And very much the point, yes!<br /> <br /> These are all loyalist character concepts I would have loved to play in Ex2 but couldn't because of the Neverborn. None of these ideas rack up the kind of world-destablizing body counts that the dead titans are interested in. If I had played one of these characters, then eventually my Deathlord patron would have told me to stop playing around and go start a plague or war somewhere, because no one is interested in half a dozen deaths when the end game is world-destruction. And it's impossible for me to empathize with a character who wants that sort of pointlessly wanton destruction, so all these characters end up boiling down to a Redemption arch, which I've played a billion times because that's really all I can do.<br /> <br /> Point is, more character options are usually a good thing. None of my examples are faultless hero archetypes, at all, but at least by transitioning from an objective of <i>slowing</i> Oblivion instead of <i>hastening</i> it, they become somewhat sympathetic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1318,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1318-kindledradial","user":"kindledRadial","date":". 04-17-2016, 03:26 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1318-kindledradial","name":"kindledRadial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1318_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T13:13:56-07:00","id":867012,"title":"","content":"In order to reduce the Gaddafi factor of the Bronze faction, how about emphasizing the fact that they're not actually in charge?<br /> <br /> In the wake of the Usurpation, maybe they could've conquered the world outright. But instead, they hid themselves and left the world-ruling to the gods and the Dragonblooded. They retained enormous influence, of course, but they never took the throne. They don't even run the Bureau of Destiny.<br /> <br /> Maybe some elder Sidereals are still a bit bitter about that.<br /> <br /> &quot;Let me tell you, the world wouldn't be in such a dismal state if we were ruling it. Have you seen the stupid things the Dragonblooded do? It's not arrogant to say that we know better, because we do. So why are they ruling the world, while we still take orders from fools like Yaogin the Fair?<br /> <br /> If Chejop and his cronies hadn't been such cowards in the aftermath of the Usurpation, we could've done a better job of it. Kept the Mask intact and seized Creation for ourselves. We could've guided humanity onto an auspicious path, and not spent centuries labouring under idiot gods.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/797631-why-craft-sucks-how-i-fixed-it\" target=\"_blank\">EX3 Craft Rewrite</a><br /> <br /> Sanctaphrax is not a person<br /> -Chejop Kejak","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1154-sanctaphrax","name":"Sanctaphrax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1154_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1460"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T14:15:48-07:00","id":867047,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Guancyto</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also why House of Cards enormously misrepresents the American political process</div> </div> </div> </div> That's what you get when you adapt a series depicting a society in which governments and administrative leaders aren't really elected, they're chosen by the internal politics of party elites. Is the story of House of Cards more outlandish than Tony Blair and Gordon Brown having a private dinner in which they discuss what order they'll be Prime Minister in?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T14:35:53-07:00","id":867063,"title":"","content":"Yes.<br /> <br /> Much.<br /> <br /> I mean goodness, those two are close enough that they have <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dQw4w9WgXcQ\" target=\"_blank\">slash fiction</a>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2144-guancyto","name":"Guancyto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"699"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T16:25:49-07:00","id":867149,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>People saying stuff about FF7 being terribad because Sephiroph was uninteresting</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Stuff!!</div> </div> </div> </div>Ugh. Go watch Crimson Peak. <br /> <br /> The ghost is a metaphor.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>(10chars)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2721-elkovash","name":"Elkovash","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2721_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"528"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T17:43:42-07:00","id":867210,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>haren</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=866800#post866800\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hey, you want more games about fighting for Social Justice, I'll be the first person standing beside you there. It's a good thing! But sometimes I also want a game where I don't have to think/feel hard on those days that just have sucked the life from me. I want to just enjoy the game's physical aspects... maybe even some minor strategy. I want a challenge, but more of a somewhat physical nature.</div> </div> </div> </div>Man, that's not the point at all. You can tell a story about playing a low-grade mugger trying to mug people, or about people trying to escape from a haunted house, or about someone trying to become the next demon king or become a billionaire or whatever. It's not like their goals need to be virtuous. What I want is for that setting and story to feel <i>real</i>, to have actual thought and depth put into it.<br /> <br /> My problem is with games that use &quot;all right, now that we've established the basics, your goal is to kill a bullshit superdemon&quot; as an excuse to not have to portray a believable world or an interesting plot, because lol, it's just mindless fantasy, the people we're selling it to won't expect any better.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","name":"Aquillion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar517_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1341"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T18:54:46-07:00","id":867276,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kindledRadial</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=866985#post866985\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And very much the point, yes!<br /> <br /> These are all loyalist character concepts I would have loved to play in Ex2 but couldn't because of the Neverborn. None of these ideas rack up the kind of world-destablizing body counts that the dead titans are interested in. If I had played one of these characters, then eventually my Deathlord patron would have told me to stop playing around and go start a plague or war somewhere, because no one is interested in half a dozen deaths when the end game is world-destruction. And it's impossible for me to empathize with a character who wants that sort of pointlessly wanton destruction, so all these characters end up boiling down to a Redemption arch, which I've played a billion times because that's really all I can do.<br /> <br /> Point is, more character options are usually a good thing. None of my examples are faultless hero archetypes, at all, but at least by transitioning from an objective of <i>slowing</i> Oblivion instead of <i>hastening</i> it, they become somewhat sympathetic.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think it'd be easier if they just didn't have to have a goal related to Oblivion at all, one way or the other.<br /> <br /> And I think it's more interesting to have any &quot;sympathetic&quot; nature be inherent to them. For example, the champion of the dead is sympathetic because he's protecting his people, not because this is part of some huge thing to subvert Oblivion.<br /> <br /> I mean, I'm not saying I hate it, I just don't think it adds a lot.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T18:56:52-07:00","id":867278,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sanctaphrax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=867012#post867012\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In order to reduce the Gaddafi factor of the Bronze faction, how about emphasizing the fact that they're not actually in charge?<br /> <br /> In the wake of the Usurpation, maybe they could've conquered the world outright. But instead, they hid themselves and left the world-ruling to the gods and the Dragonblooded. They retained enormous influence, of course, but they never took the throne. They don't even run the Bureau of Destiny.<br /> <br /> Maybe some elder Sidereals are still a bit bitter about that.<br /> <br /> &quot;Let me tell you, the world wouldn't be in such a dismal state if we were ruling it. Have you seen the stupid things the Dragonblooded do? It's not arrogant to say that we know better, because we do. So why are they ruling the world, while we still take orders from fools like Yaogin the Fair?<br /> <br /> If Chejop and his cronies hadn't been such cowards in the aftermath of the Usurpation, we could've done a better job of it. Kept the Mask intact and seized Creation for ourselves. We could've guided humanity onto an auspicious path, and not spent centuries labouring under idiot gods.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>I feel a better way to have them not be monsters who claim to be doing it for the greater good is to show some examples of them doing good stuff. Some stories about Bronze Faction defending small towns from rampaging barbarian hordes led by Lunas, etc.<br /> <br /> (Personally, I always found Kejak and the Bronze Faction fine as villains/heroes. I don't really see what the big issue is.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T19:28:26-07:00","id":867292,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=867276#post867276\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I mean, I'm not saying I hate it, I just don't think it adds a lot.</div> </div> </div> </div>Or anything, really.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T20:01:50-07:00","id":867309,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=867278#post867278\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I feel a better way to have them not be monsters who claim to be doing it for the greater good is to show some examples of them doing good stuff. Some stories about Bronze Faction defending small towns from rampaging barbarian hordes led by Lunas, etc.<br /> <br /> (Personally, I always found Kejak and the Bronze Faction fine as villains/heroes. I don't really see what the big issue is.)</div> </div> </div> </div>The issue is that, despite being arguably good guys, they spout the rhetoric of dictators to justify committing the crimes of dictators. And we're expected to buy that rhetoric. It's as if the Bull of the North's campaign was just as atrocity-filled as Genghis Khan's, and we were expected to believe him when he said<br /> <br /> <b>\u201cI am the punishment of God...If you had not committed great sins, God would not have sent a punishment like me upon you.\u201d</b><br /> <br /> Except a bit more contemporary, because dictators spouting hard decisions BS are still around today.<br /> <br /> Having them do good things doesn't do anything to address that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/797631-why-craft-sucks-how-i-fixed-it\" target=\"_blank\">EX3 Craft Rewrite</a><br /> <br /> Sanctaphrax is not a person<br /> -Chejop Kejak","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1154-sanctaphrax","name":"Sanctaphrax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1154_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1460"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T20:09:14-07:00","id":867313,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> they spout the rhetoric of dictators to justify committing the crimes of dictators </div> </div> </div>Could you give me some examples??<br /> <br /> (Though if you're going to commit mass murder and wipe out a ruling class, I can't see how else you're going to justify it other than &quot;they oppressed us&quot; and &quot;it was for the greater good&quot;.)<br /> <br /> And I'm not sure you are supposed to buy that rhetoric completely. It's just one perspective.<br /> I mean, one could equally argue that you're supposed to buy into the Solars' presentation as the rightful rulers and that the Bronze Faction are pure evil for overthrowing the rightful kings. But again, that's just one perspective.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T20:36:59-07:00","id":867329,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=867313#post867313\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Could you give me some examples??<br /> <br /> (Though if you're going to commit mass murder and wipe out a ruling class, I can't see how else you're going to justify it other than &quot;they oppressed us&quot; and &quot;it was for the greater good&quot;.)<br /> <br /> And I'm not sure you are supposed to buy that rhetoric completely. It's just one perspective.<br /> I mean, one could equally argue that you're supposed to buy into the Solars' presentation as the rightful rulers and that the Bronze Faction are pure evil for overthrowing the rightful kings. But again, that's just one perspective.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Except they didn't just murder the ruling class. They committed genocide on who knows how many people that MIGHT have been a problem. Child of a Solar? Gotta die. A type of humanity given shape by a Solar a millenium ago? Gotta die. Sidereals who don't want to be a part of it, but aren't looking like they'll tell? Gotta also die, because they MIGHT tell. It's not about killing 300 people. It's about the &quot;collateral damage&quot; they thought was totally acceptable. Now, most Bronze Faction Sids I figure weren't part of that or bought into Kejack's cult of personality. He was the person in charge.<br /> <br /> And for the record, look at a number of &quot;rebellions&quot; where they kill the ruling class (ever hear of The French Revolution?), then murder everyone who might not be 100% with the program (or worse). It's a pretty common theme in history, and in the end the buck stops at Kejack's door.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />\u200bWhen noise turns to silence, when colors dull and pale, when reality no longer makes sense, there shall you find me. There, in the dreams of the River of Faceless Millions, do I dwell.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/\" target=\"_blank\">http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/</a> for my art.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","name":"haren","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar378_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"664"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T20:42:19-07:00","id":867332,"title":"","content":"They overthrew the government, seized power, instituted a policy of genocide (in 2e, at least), made themselves completely unaccountable to their subjects, spread enormous amounts of false propaganda, and set themselves up as the ultimate secret police enforcing the ultimate secret laws.<br /> <br /> But it's okay, they claim, because they judge it to be necessary. And they are wise and strong and trustworthy. It is important for the good of Creation that when a Sidereal kills your brother, she be allowed to return unharmed to her diamond-studded pleasure palace in Heaven. After all, she wouldn't have done it if it didn't need to happen.<br /> <br /> I wouldn't like to see any &quot;rightful king&quot; BS in Exalted either.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/797631-why-craft-sucks-how-i-fixed-it\" target=\"_blank\">EX3 Craft Rewrite</a><br /> <br /> Sanctaphrax is not a person<br /> -Chejop Kejak","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1154-sanctaphrax","name":"Sanctaphrax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1154_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1460"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T20:45:29-07:00","id":867334,"title":"","content":"I'm not saying it doesn't, I just don't see what the problem is in terms of the games' presentation. It seems to me that Kejak is presented in much the same way as other revolutionary leaders; Mao, Lenin, etc. Collateral damage is acceptable as part of the revolution. My point isn't that it shows Kejak as good, but is he supposed to be shown as good? Not really.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T20:51:48-07:00","id":867340,"title":"","content":"Like you said, the Bronze faction is a bunch of villain/heroes. They do some good things.<br /> <br /> If Chejop is basically Mao, then the whole &quot;is the Bronze faction right&quot; thing becomes almost insulting.<br /> <br /> I think having the Bronzes very clearly not actually rule the world would do a lot to differentiate them from scum like Mao.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/797631-why-craft-sucks-how-i-fixed-it\" target=\"_blank\">EX3 Craft Rewrite</a><br /> <br /> Sanctaphrax is not a person<br /> -Chejop Kejak","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1154-sanctaphrax","name":"Sanctaphrax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1154_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1460"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T21:27:10-07:00","id":867359,"title":"","content":"I'd point out that, here in China, Mao is still considered the great Helmsman who saved China from the Japanese and Chiang Kai-Shek's corrupt and parasitical regime (director of the White Terror, in case people forget). And that is semi-true. Now, does that make Mao a good guy? No, not at all. And not only was he a bad guy, but his economic incompetence killed millions upon millions.<br /> But while there are many Chinese who, when they hear the details of everything he did,* think he's a monster whose crimes were covered up, there are others who say &quot;well, he did bad things sure, but he was still a strong leader who saved China.&quot; Who is right? I'd say the former view, not the latter, but my point is that, if Chejop is like Mao, there are going to be people in the setting who defend what he did: yes, he was bad, they'll say, but better than the Solars. And their arguments are not based on nothing but lies.<br /> <br /> *And it's not like many people don't know a fair bit, it's just filtered through propaganda.<br /> <br /> I mean, I wouldn't actually concentrate on Mao specifically (as the revolutionary leader with the biggest body count), but rather the general thing of revolutionary leaders.<br /> <br /> (Lenin's probably a better example: first a brutal civil war to kill the supporters of the former regime, followed by more moderate but still very problematic policies. Though you could consider the Bronze Faction's failure to foresee the Great Contagion as akin to Mao's incompetence vis a vis the Great Leap Forward: millions died, but this wasn't a deliberate attempt to kill people, just as the Sidereals didn't realise the Great Contagion would be the ultimate result of the Usurpation)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T21:34:08-07:00","id":867363,"title":"","content":"We're not in the setting, though. And we're supposed to take the &quot;it was necessary&quot; line seriously. It's not supposed to be the obviously self-serving crap that that line pretty much always is in real life.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/797631-why-craft-sucks-how-i-fixed-it\" target=\"_blank\">EX3 Craft Rewrite</a><br /> <br /> Sanctaphrax is not a person<br /> -Chejop Kejak","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1154-sanctaphrax","name":"Sanctaphrax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1154_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1460"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T23:25:08-07:00","id":867389,"title":"","content":"A failure of Dreams of the First Age, I think. When the days leading up to the Usurpation were just mostly undefined as an &quot;Age of Nightmares&quot; your imagination could fill in the blanks, and if you wanted to take the &quot;it was necessary&quot; line seriously you could think of all manner of ways in which the Solars were the worst and the fall of civilization was needed.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, if you're told it was a post-scarcity sci-fi paradise with mild horror undertones, just run by people who were sort of assholes (but who are not actually as bad as many people we laud in the real world), the necessity of burning it all to the ground is sort of laughable?<br /> <br /> Edit: I mean, there's even a fairly clear &quot;right&quot; answer to the Bronze vs. Gold prophecies with things as presented in DotFA: you Bronzekill all the elders, then Goldmentor the younger generation who hated them anyway, and Jadeprison exactly nothing. It's not a strategy without problems, but a question of global revolution should never have an optimum solution!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2144,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2144-guancyto","user":"Guancyto","date":". 04-18-2016, 02:34 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2144-guancyto","name":"Guancyto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"699"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T00:18:16-07:00","id":867404,"title":"","content":"I'm honestly wondering if the whole &quot;Oh, we took over the world for the Greater Good, because our Prophetic Powers mean we obviously know what's best for everyone for all time&quot; thing the Sidereals have was part of the point. Like, the Scarlet Empire was a critique of Qin Shi Huang and legalism, so I don't really have much trouble seeing that Chejop might well have been written as a critique of the military dictator or something. But, just, maybe not expanded upon and explained perfectly so that comes across well.<br /> <br /> I suspect things will work better in 3e, since, on the one hand, we'll have lots of problems that only the Sidereals are really handling and may well be the only ones really equipped to handle them, but, on the other, we'll have people like Rakan Thulio and the Getimeans who are railing about the oppressive injustice of it all and that shows us the other side of things. So, the seeds are there for a more nuanced approach to the Sidereals in this edition and we can hope they execute on it well.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>....","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/226-brilliantrain","name":"BrilliantRain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar226_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3718"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T00:42:26-07:00","id":867418,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sanctaphrax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=867363#post867363\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">We're not in the setting, though. And we're supposed to take the &quot;it was necessary&quot; line seriously. It's not supposed to be the obviously self-serving crap that that line pretty much always is in real life.</div> </div> </div> </div>Are we? Says who? I mean, I could say a lot of the books push the view that &quot;it wasn't necessary and led to the Great Contagion; Solars are totally awesome and rad&quot;.<br /> I also don't think you can say &quot;obviously self-serving crap that that line pretty much always is in real life.&quot; When the Bolsheviks murdered the Russian royal family, children and all, most of them really believed it was necessary to help end the civil war (with the right side winning: the right side obviously being the communists). They didn't do it just to steal their jewels or something.<br /> <br /> The books present some bad stuff first age Solars did. You can decide if it was necessary yourself. Or, more importantly, characters can decide. So Solars are likely to say it was unnecessary, while Dragonblood are likely to say it was. And this is just the same as in real life, where people talk about ancient rulers and argue about how justified their atrocities were. As a British person, I hear this all the time about British history. &quot;Oh, sure, our Empire killed some people, but overall it was a good thing in the long run.&quot; That's what I'd expect Sidereals to say.<br /> <br /> Personally, I'd much rather have the books say &quot;Chejop Kejak and co did this because they believed it was necessary&quot; than &quot;Chejop Kejak and co did this because they're evil dickwads who live evil&quot; because I've already got the Deathlords for that (and that's boring too).<br /> <br /> Bad people often believe themselves to be good people. They justify their bad stuff not only to others, but also to themselves. And I think that makes them more interesting villains for the game.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T00:43:57-07:00","id":867420,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BrilliantRain</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=867404#post867404\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm honestly wondering if the whole &quot;Oh, we took over the world for the Greater Good, because our Prophetic Powers mean we obviously know what's best for everyone for all time&quot; thing the Sidereals have was part of the point. Like, the Scarlet Empire was a critique of Qin Shi Huang and legalism, so I don't really have much trouble seeing that Chejop might well have been written as a critique of the military dictator or something. But, just, maybe not expanded upon and explained perfectly so that comes across well.<br /> <br /> I suspect things will work better in 3e, since, on the one hand, we'll have lots of problems that only the Sidereals are really handling and may well be the only ones really equipped to handle them, but, on the other, we'll have people like Rakan Thulio and the Getimeans who are railing about the oppressive injustice of it all and that shows us the other side of things. So, the seeds are there for a more nuanced approach to the Sidereals in this edition and we can hope they execute on it well.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree. I'd like to see opposition to the Bronze Faction be based around more than &quot;Solars are awesome, stop oppressing them.&quot; There's plenty of other reasons to oppose them.<br /> The books did mention a bit that many Gods dislike them because of the Immaculate Order, at least.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T00:51:09-07:00","id":867423,"title":"","content":"Thinking about it some more not sure the Bronze Faction singles out for me as a particularly easy analogy to Qaddafi and to real life coups and revolutions justified as necessary, in setting, given pretty much all of the Exalted have been and are involved in &quot;It's necessary&quot; coups of one form or another in the setting at some point. <br /> <br /> I guess the objection here is mostly that the book is asking us to take the Sidereals' choice more at face value, while elsewhere is letting us take a cynical point of view towards the &quot;It's necessary&quot; rhetoric of the Lunars and Solars trying to replace the Dragonblooded Realm, or the gods towards the Primordials (and however the Solars seized power in the First Age anyway)?<br /> <br /> (If anything the rhetoric Qaddafi, and other recent dictators like him, used was about his coup being necessary to liberate his nation from foreign imperialist interests, which is probably less like anything the Sidereals would use than many other Exalted.)<br /> <br /> (As another aside, more than dictators, to the extent there is any contemporary parallel, the CIA often seemed like the *right* analogy for the Sidereals. Or at least, the CIA as imagined by Russian propagandists, more than the real CIA which seems a bit like that but not near as capable or competent and based on leaks (which may be dubious) often sincerely obsessed with using secret Liberal Interventionism to spread democracy / impress their bosses. Or the KGB, if that works better.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T01:07:01-07:00","id":867428,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure it does present the Sidereals' choice more at face value than it does the Solars'. Rather each is a different perspective.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T03:57:07-07:00","id":867474,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sanctaphrax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=867012#post867012\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In order to reduce the Gaddafi factor of the Bronze faction, how about emphasizing the fact that they're not actually in charge?</div> </div> </div> </div>That's the route I've historically taken.<br /> <br /> Specifically Chejop Kejak as the one enforcing the policies of &quot;the Dragon-Blooded rule Creation, not us&quot; and &quot;the Solars are a threat to the world's future but we don't actively hate them&quot; with a group of followers that doesn't always follow through on these ideals because of a combination of the Great Curse and human nature.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T11:45:28-07:00","id":867705,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=867418#post867418\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As a British person, I hear this all the time about British history. &quot;Oh, sure, our Empire killed some people, but overall it was a good thing in the long run.&quot; That's what I'd expect Sidereals to say.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's what I'd expect Deathlord sympathizers to say.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/797631-why-craft-sucks-how-i-fixed-it\" target=\"_blank\">EX3 Craft Rewrite</a><br /> <br /> Sanctaphrax is not a person<br /> -Chejop Kejak","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1154-sanctaphrax","name":"Sanctaphrax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1154_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1460"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T18:45:20-07:00","id":868024,"title":"","content":"Probably some do (though, actually, not most: these are mostly people who think killing people is good, so they're not going to say &quot;unfortunately we had to kill a lot of people for the greater good&quot; because killing isn't unfortunate to them&quot;). But I don't see that as being a big problem for the game. Would it be better for them to go &quot;bwahaha, we're so evil?&quot; Seems kind of dull.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T19:44:05-07:00","id":868078,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=867474#post867474\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's the route I've historically taken.<br /> <br /> Specifically Chejop Kejak as the one enforcing the policies of &quot;the Dragon-Blooded rule Creation, not us&quot; and &quot;the Solars are a threat to the world's future but we don't actively hate them&quot; with a group of followers that doesn't always follow through on these ideals because of a combination of the Great Curse and human nature.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> My head canon is that Kejak doesn't hate Solars, he fears them. He regrets the necessity of killing them, but the idea of a Solar running loose in Creation chills him to the bone.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T20:59:33-07:00","id":868146,"title":"","content":"I agree, I never particularly got the feeling that he fanatically hated Solars. His DotFA background didn't have him losing a relative to the Solars or anything. It's a colder, more analytical thing, based on his fear of how the world would become with Solars.<br /> <br /> It also makes him overly-focused: his fear of Solars blinds him to the danger of other threats relative to Solars. Even with his intelligence and foresight, he was so busy worrying about the Solars, and, secondarily, the Lunars, that he never realised what a danger the Deathlords were. It's only dawning on him now that they're such a threat, and that's a lot to do with the fact they have Abyssals.<br /> Similarly, he wasn't concerned about what the Yozis were up to: Yozis are, to him, far less worrying than Solars.<br /> <br /> Of course, that's his second ed portrayal. With Getimians around, the Deathlords not quite so apocalyptic, the Yozis not about to escape, it's rather different. Both that he's likely less mono-focused, and it's less of an issue because he's not ignoring 90% of the 1000 Dooms.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T01:40:06-07:00","id":868275,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=867276#post867276\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think it'd be easier if they just didn't have to have a goal related to Oblivion at all, one way or the other.<br /> <br /> And I think it's more interesting to have any &quot;sympathetic&quot; nature be inherent to them. For example, the champion of the dead is sympathetic because he's protecting his people, not because this is part of some huge thing to subvert Oblivion.<br /> <br /> I mean, I'm not saying I hate it, I just don't think it adds a lot.</div> </div> </div> </div>You certainly could, did you have a specific alternative in mind?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1318-kindledradial","name":"kindledRadial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1318_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T10:44:00-07:00","id":868567,"title":"","content":"One of the ways I would shift the roles of the Deathknights and Deathlords is to say that you can't actually serve Oblivion, and you certainly can't derive power from it. Oblivion doesn't put anything in, only remove it. The essence of the Dead, then, is the stuff of preservation, stagnation, finality, and endings that are not endings. That is to say, it is the Essence that maintains the perpetual Underworld.<br /> <br /> That's not to say that the Neverborn don't crave Oblivion, or that Nephwracks can't work towards a day (or lack therof) where everything (or lack thereof) is annihilated in Oblivion. But those are their motivations, and their essence itself carries a different allegiance. The secret that the Deathlords might not know (given their embrace of melodrama) and certainly don't tell their Abyssals is that the choice to end the world is not made once and regretted forever after, it is a constant thing made moment by moment that can always be turned away from.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T12:01:54-07:00","id":868636,"title":"","content":"I'm inclined to divorce the Deathlords from being straight-up servants of the Neverborn and universally interested in promoting Oblivion (in theory). I think it allows them more freedom if they have merely, say, been touched by the essence of the Neverborn, or plundered their tombs for power, and were changed in the process, each one affected to different degrees. So you can have one who declares themselves enemies to oblivion and the Neverborn, one who does believe in the former to a similar degree as Nephwracks, one who's just interested in building up and preserving their realm in the Underworld, one who wishes to develop and build up the Underworld, one whose interests lie in covering all of Creation in Shadowlands...<br /> <br /> The essence of the Dead/Death, which empowers the Abyssals and has mighty icons in the form of the Deathlords, as something that maintains the perpetual Underworld, resonant with preservation, remembrance, and endings that are not endings, is something that has been suggested before, and I continue to find it a very workable and interesting concept. The developers and writers obviously have their own thoughts which we are not privy to, but it sounds like a plausible possibility. I wouldn't mind seeing that take become official.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":188,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/188-yeoflittlefaith","user":"YeOfLittleFaith","date":". 04-19-2016, 03:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/188-yeoflittlefaith","name":"YeOfLittleFaith","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar188_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-22T14:41:45-07:00","id":871744,"title":"","content":"I think any change to the Deathlords or Abyssals is always ultimately going to be strained because the setting still demands they're bound by the will of the Neverborn, which demands they work towards the overall Big Bad. So I think the Abyssals, and even the Deathlords, could be made a whole lot more relatable, and even playable with a clearer conscious, by making one simple change to the Neverborn:<br /> <br /> Switch &quot;The Neverborn want to kill off the the entire world so they can truly die&quot; to just &quot;The Neverborn want to truly die&quot;.<br /> <br /> Take away the requirement that everything else has to die so they can go away, and everything about them and those they empower changes. What if the Neverborn simply don't know how to finally end their existence? Suddenly the setting changes from Lovecraftian entities trying to bring about the end to all things to Lovecraftian entities using formerly human entities as imperfect intermediaries in a search for their own personal oblivion. They were pseudo-killed during the Primordial war, cosmic beings forced into a state of existence so alien to everything before this point that they created a whole new realm in their wake. Now they're probably in a state of constant agony, completely unable to connect to the world they once ruled, and want nothing more than just to end their own pain. Suddenly the Usurpation happens, and the new realm they created is suddenly filled with the ghosts of the beings that were the closest to them when they 'lived', the ones that 'killed' them. But hey, water under the bridge, if anyone can figure out how to finish the job they started, it's their killers, so they reach out, and a few of the solar ghosts hear them, make some sense of their agonized whispers, and are sympathetic, or see an opportunity for power, or see some moral necessity in the eventual death of the Neverborn.<br /> <br /> So now the Deathlords aren't harbingers of the death of everything, but rather more priests, scholars, and conquerers trying to root out the secrets of life and death so that they can bring about their masters' desired end. Of course the whispers of pseudo-dead primordials filtered through their constant agony is anything but clear, no one Deathlord can agree with any of the others how exactly the Neverborn want them to go about finding the secret to their ultimate death. This introduces the potential for a lot more variety in the Deathlords' aims. Sure, some can interpret the whispers as a desire to end the universe, and seek it for reasons ranging from suicidal nihilism to the moral conviction that the Neverborn were wronged and deserve their release even if it means the death of everything else. Others might believe releasing the Neverborn involves recreating the Primordial War that created them in the first place, and see the amassing of ghosts and eventual creation of the Abyssals as steps towards a great but necessary battle to truly 'kill' the Neverborn. Still others might think that finding and control the very nature of Essence might allow them to forcibly alter reality and create new laws that allow for the death of the Neverborn. Or maybe some think the answer lies in subjugating the Wyld and forcing the agents of chaos and potential to create a way for the Neverborn to die.<br /> <br /> Most of the history of the Underworld and its interactions with Creation can remain the same, the intentions merely change. The conflicts between the Deathlords are now over philosophical/theological differences. The release of the Great Contagion could possibly be an attempt by the Duchess to open up a passage to a new reality for the Neverborn to travel to and actually die gone awry, or possibly an experiment by her to create a sickness that could kill even undead gods that got released into Creation. Now the Abyssals can have any number of drives and moral standings and still be bound to the Deathlords and the Neverborn. They could believe the deaths they cause are necessary to finally release the Neverborn from torment. Or they act against a Deathlord who follows the annihilation-of-all philosophy and believe any number of deaths is worth averting the end of everything, much the same as the Bronze Faction just in a different context. Or maybe they believe the Underworld is full of sentient beings who deserve the right to continue their existence, and seek a way to balance the needs of the living and the dead together.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11640-djinnkitty83","name":"Djinnkitty83","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-22T15:00:33-07:00","id":871762,"title":"","content":"The setting really doesn't ultimately demand the Deathlords be bound by the will of the Neverborn. I just finished reading Clarke Ashton Smith's <i>The Return of the Sorcerer</i> and man that book is <i>chock full</i> of sorcerers and necromancers who get their power from the patronage of some fell and alien power but do not give a single fuck about actually doing that fell and alien power's will.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":10}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/229-stephen-lea-sheppard","name":"Stephen Lea Sheppard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar229_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-22T15:21:57-07:00","id":871781,"title":"","content":"I recall some interesting thoughts<a href=\"http://exalted.xi.co.nz/wiki/Thus_Spake_Zaraborgstrom/DeathlordsPlan\" target=\"_blank\">http://exalted.xi.co.nz/wiki/Thus_Sp...DeathlordsPlan</a> which Jenna Moran had on this very subject. (With any luck, I didn't miss this upthread, causing me to make a fool of myself now.)<br /> <br /> With regards to the Abyssals themselves, I have biases. Largely because I really LIKE the idea of a character with powers which can mostly only destroy things trying to accomplish other things. To me, that's super-compelling, but it's probably too narrow to really tie a whole splat to.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;For me, there's no fundamental conflict between really loving something and also seeing it as very profoundly flawed.&quot; -- Jay Eddidin","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2168-chejop-kejak","name":"Chejop Kejak","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2168_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1219"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-23T09:08:51-07:00","id":872424,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chejop Kejak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/abyssal-exalted/851941-what-if-abyssals-were-fighting-oblivion-all-along?p=871781#post871781\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">With regards to the Abyssals themselves, I have biases. Largely because I really LIKE the idea of a character with powers which can mostly only destroy things trying to accomplish other things. To me, that's super-compelling, but it's probably too narrow to really tie a whole splat to.</div> </div> </div> </div>I also very much enjoy this theme for Abyssals, and would like to see it continued in some form in Ex3.<br /> <br /> However, I think it's implementation in Ex2 was heavy-handed, and ended up shoving all other narratives out of the picture. Resonance was the biggest offender there, with mechanics that actively penalized player choices that weren't destructive, and then produced wanton destruction over time even if you tried to be good. The charmset was a better place to enforce and experiment with that theme, but then &quot;destruction&quot; so often meant &quot;senseless murder&quot; that it started to feel like an entire splat for playing Freddy Krueger. I think slasher flicks should be a valid inspiration for Abyssals, but I would hope that their charm set would allow them to pursue other concepts. And after trying and <i>failing</i> to do <i>any</i> good over and over again, I for one have a hard time justifying the hero hashtag for my characters.<br /> <br /> As an idea, if &quot;justified sacrifices&quot; was more of a mechanic for Abyssals I'd feel like that theme was getting used better. Like if instead of &quot;the Abyssal kills puppies, but may kill 10,000 puppies if you start by eating one&quot; we had &quot;the Abyssal saves a puppy, but must kill another puppy at a later date to balance out these gains&quot; I would have an easier time justifying a character who saw themselves as at least partially good in this setting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1318-kindledradial","name":"kindledRadial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1318_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"487"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}